---
import Site from '../layouts/Site.astro';
import Walkthrough from '../components/Walkthrough.astro';
---

<Site title="Connect your AI assistant to your apps">
  <main id="main">
    <section class="hero container">
      <div class="hero-copy">
        <p class="eyebrow">
          <span class="status-dot"></span> Connect AI to your apps{' '}
          <span class="alpha-tag">v0.1 alpha</span>
        </p>
        <h1>
          Your AI.
          <br />
          Your apps.
          <br />
          <em>Your rules.</em>
        </h1>
        <p class="hero-description">
          Blindkit connects your AI assistant to your apps. Your AI never needs your API keys. You
          choose which actions can run and which need your approval.
        </p>
        <div class="hero-actions">
          <a class="button" href="/docs/quickstart/">
            Get started <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
          </a>
          <a class="text-link" href="/docs/">
            Read the docs <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
          </a>
        </div>
        <p class="hero-note mono">On your computer · Open source · No account needed</p>
      </div>
      <div class="hero-demo">
        <div class="demo-caption">
          <span class="mono">See what happens before a change</span>
          <span aria-hidden="true">↙</span>
        </div>
        <Walkthrough />
      </div>
    </section>
    <section id="how-it-works" class="section container">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">How it works</p>
        <h2>
          Connect an app.
          <br />
          Let your AI help.
        </h2>
        <p>
          Ask your AI to look something up or make a change. Blindkit checks your rules before
          sending the request to the app.
        </p>
      </div>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li>
          <span class="step-number mono">01 / Connect</span>
          <h3>Connect an app.</h3>
          <p>
            Add an OpenAPI file that describes what the app can do. Blindkit turns those actions
            into tools for your AI.
          </p>
          <code>Connect once</code>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="step-number mono">02 / Discover</span>
          <h3>Ask your AI to help.</h3>
          <p>
            Your assistant finds the action it needs and asks Blindkit to run it. For example, it
            can read a post or create a new one.
          </p>
          <code>“Show me the first post.”</code>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="step-number mono">03 / Decide</span>
          <h3>Choose what is allowed.</h3>
          <p>
            Let an action run, ask to review it first, or block it. When approval is needed, your AI
            has to wait for your decision.
          </p>
          <code>Allow · Ask first · Block</code>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>
    <section class="boundary-section">
      <div class="container boundary-grid">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">Your keys. Your decision.</p>
          <h2>
            Your AI can help.
            <br />
            You stay in charge.
          </h2>
          <p>
            Blindkit sends requests for your AI. It checks the details, applies your rules, and adds
            API keys on your computer.
          </p>
          <a class="light-link" href="/docs/security/">
            Read how protection works <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
          </a>
        </div>
        <dl>
          <div>
            <dt>Limits on what can run</dt>
            <dd>
              AI-written code runs in a separate space with limits on time, memory, and the number
              of requests.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Ask before making changes</dt>
            <dd>
              Changes need your approval by default. A blocked action stays blocked, even if your AI
              asks to run it.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>See what happened</dt>
            <dd>
              Blindkit saves a request before sending it, then records the result. If it cannot save
              the request, it does not send it.
            </dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="section container getting-started">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Start small</p>
        <h2>
          Start with one app.
          <br />
          Run it on your computer.
        </h2>
        <p>
          Follow the setup guide and try the included example. This early version needs a terminal
          and an AI app that supports MCP, the standard for connecting AI to tools.
        </p>
        <a class="text-link" href="/docs/quickstart/">
          Follow the setup guide <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
        </a>
      </div>
      <div class="terminal">
        <div class="terminal-bar">
          <span class="mono">Run in your Blindkit folder</span>
          <span class="terminal-dots" aria-hidden="true">
            ● ● ●
          </span>
        </div>
        <pre><code>pnpm install --frozen-lockfile
pnpm build
node dist/cli.js init
node dist/cli.js add \
  examples/posts.openapi.json --name posts</code></pre>
        <p>You’ll need Node 22 or 24 and pnpm 10. The guide explains each step.</p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="container faq-section">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Before you connect</p>
        <h2>A few clear answers.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="faq">
        <details>
          <summary>Is Blindkit ready for important work?</summary>
          <p>
            This is an early release for trying things out. Automated tests pass, but an outside
            security review and testing with more AI apps are still needed. Read the{' '}
            <a href="/docs/security/">security guide</a> before giving it access to important data.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Which apps can I connect?</summary>
          <p>
            You need an app with an OpenAPI 3.0 JSON file describing its API. Some formats and
            sign-in methods are not supported yet. Check the{' '}
            <a href="/docs/config/">supported formats</a>.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Does my AI see my API keys?</summary>
          <p>
            Blindkit keeps the keys you configure on your computer and hides known keys from
            returned data. This does not hide every possible secret or private detail. Only connect
            apps and an AI client you trust.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>What if my AI app cannot ask for approval?</summary>
          <p>
            Actions you have allowed still work. An action that needs approval is stopped if your AI
            app cannot show the approval form. Your AI cannot approve itself.
          </p>
        </details>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="closing container">
      <span class="eyebrow">Start with a simple example.</span>
      <h2>Put your AI to work. Set the rules.</h2>
      <a class="button" href="/docs/quickstart/">
        Get started <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
      </a>
    </section>
  </main>
</Site>
